  "worked_solution": "**Step 1**: We need to find the value of $z$ such that the manufacturing cost\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =5\\cdot 2xz+2\\cdot 2xy+2\\cdot 2yz \\\\  & =10xz+4xy+4yz\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis the smallest possible value.\n\n**Step 2**: The volume must be $50\\,{ft}^{3}.$ So, we have the constraint equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =x\\cdot z\\cdot y \\\\ 50 & =3{z}^{2}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We make $y$ the subject of the constraint equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{z}^{2}y & =50 \\\\ y & =\\frac{50}{3{z}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute the above into our expression for $C,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =10xz+4xy+4yz \\\\ C & =10(3z)z+4(3z)(\\frac{50}{3{z}^{2}})+4(\\frac{50}{3{z}^{2}})z \\\\ C & =30{z}^{2}+\\frac{200}{z}+\\frac{200}{3z} \\\\ C & =30{z}^{2}+\\frac{800}{3z}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: We solve the equation ${C}^{′}(z)=0.$ Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{C}^{′}(z)=60z-\\frac{800}{3{z}^{2}},\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}^{′}(z) & =0 \\\\ 60z-\\frac{800}{3{z}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 180{z}^{3} & =800 \\\\ {z}^{3} & =\\frac{800}{180} \\\\ z & =\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{40}{9}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: We confirm that $z=\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{40}{9}}$ is a minimum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{C}^{″}(z)=60+\\frac{1\\,600}{{z}^{3}}\\,⟹\\,{C}^{″}(\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{40}{9}})>0.\n$$\n\nSince ${C}^{″}(\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{40}{9}})$ is positive, we conclude that $z=\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{40}{9}}$ is indeed a minimum of $C(z).$",
